{"core": "Abstraction pulls particulars up into patterns — the specific dissolves into the general, and the general becomes usable thought. In Wisdom, it works on what is quietly held: the deep, unspoken knowing that waits without needing to explain itself. Underdone, the held knowing never lifts into form — it stays felt but shapeless, present but untransmittable, and the pattern that could have traveled stays locked where no one else can reach it.", "sheetLine": "The knowing sits there, real but wordless, and nothing carries it forward."}
